# Overview
hs-sensor-shield module for netconfd (yuma123): Raspberry Pi implementation of
the ietf-hardware-state YANG module (RFC 8348) for the Heimonen Solutions sensor
shield. The data model is ietf-hardware-state, the same as the other nodes, the
netconfd module (SIL) is named hs-sensor-shield.

Reports the following /hardware components:

 * raspberry-pi  - ianahw:container, serial number and model read from the device tree

 ianahw:sensor components (parent raspberry-pi), all with value-scale milli:

 name          sensor    value-type   units-display      value
 temperature   SHT41     celsius      milli degrees      degrees C x 1000
 humidity      SHT41     percent-RH   milli percent RH   %RH x 1000
 pm1           PMS7003   other        PM1 particles      ug/m3 x 1000 (atmospheric)
 pm25          PMS7003   other        PM25 particles     ug/m3 x 1000
 pm10          PMS7003   other        PM10 particles     ug/m3 x 1000
 co2           SCD41     other        milli ppm          ppm x 1000
 voc-index     SGP41     other        milli VOC index    index x 1000
 nox-index     SGP41     other        milli NOx index    index x 1000
 pressure      LPS22HB   other        milli hPa          hPa x 1000
 illuminance   BH1750    other        milli lux          lux x 1000

Each sensor component also carries <mfg-name> and <model-name> of the sensor
that produced the value. When no SHT41 is detected, temperature and humidity
are served from the SCD41's built-in temperature/humidity sensor (+-0.8 C,
+-6 %RH instead of +-0.1 C, +-1 %RH) and their <model-name> reads SCD41.

voc-index and nox-index are Sensirion gas indices (1..500), not concentrations:
100 is the baseline learned from the previous ~24 h, higher is worse air than
the baseline. They are 0 for the first 45 s after start; the VOC baseline needs
~1 h and the NOx baseline ~5 h of operation before the values are meaningful.
The NOx hotplate is conditioned for 10 s after start as required by the SGP41.

The sensors are sampled continuously by the module:

 * an I2C thread samples the SHT41, SGP41, SCD41, LPS22HB and BH1750 once per
   second. The SGP41 raw signals are converted with the Sensirion gas index
   algorithm (requires 1 Hz sampling), compensated with the SHT41 temperature
   and humidity (or the SCD41's when there is no SHT41). The SCD41 runs in
   periodic mode (a sample every 5 s).
 * a UART thread reads the PMS7003 frames.

For the first 60 s after the module starts every sensor is reported with
<oper-status>unavailable</oper-status> and no <value>, the sensors need that
time to initialise after power up (SGP41 conditioning and gas index start up,
SCD41 first samples, PMS7003 fan spin up).

After every (re)start of the SCD41's periodic measurement its self heating
compensation ramps up for minutes and its temperature/humidity read ~2 C
high: for 5 minutes after a (re)start they are neither reported (fallback)
nor used to compensate the SGP41. Every SCD41 bus error and (re)start is
logged to the netconfd log (log-level info); periodic measurement is only
restarted after 10 s of consecutive bus errors or 30 s without a sample.

The system maintenance jobs (apt-daily, man-db, fstrim, logrotate, ...) heat
up the board; on the nodes their systemd timers are all moved to 00:00 (see
below) and the module does not report the heat sensitive measurements
(temperature, humidity, voc-index, nox-index) during a daily quiet window,
00:00-01:00 local time by default. co2, pm*, pressure and illuminance are
reported as usual. The window is set with the environment variable
HS_SENSOR_SHIELD_QUIET_WINDOW=HH:MM-HH:MM (may wrap midnight), empty to
disable.

A <get> of /hardware returns the latest readings. A sensor whose last good
reading is older than 5 s (15 s for the SCD41, 10 s for the PMS7003) or that
was never found is reported with <oper-status>unavailable</oper-status> and no
<value>. Sensors that are not found are probed again every 10 s.

# Wiring
 I2C sensors: SDA -> GPIO 2, SCL -> GPIO 3 (i2c1, 100 kHz default)
 PMS7003:     TX  -> GPIO 15 (RXD, /dev/serial0), 5V, GND

The LPS22HB address depends on its SA0 pin and the BH1750 address on its ADDR
pin; both alternatives are probed (the LPS22HB is identified by WHO_AM_I first,
the BH1750 takes the other address).

# Raspberry Pi configuration
With raspi-config (Interface Options): enable I2C, enable the serial port
hardware and disable the login shell over serial. Non-interactively:

 sudo raspi-config nonint do_i2c 0
 sudo raspi-config nonint do_serial_hw 0
 sudo raspi-config nonint do_serial_cons 1
 sudo reboot

which results in /boot/firmware/config.txt containing
 dtparam=i2c_arm=on
 enable_uart=1
and no console=serial0,... in /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt.

The BH1750 DVI (reset / I2C reference) pin is wired to GPIO23 on the shield and
must be driven high, add to /boot/firmware/config.txt:
 gpio=23=op,dh

Note that the SGP41 (0x59) does not show up in i2cdetect (it NACKs the empty
probe) although it works; the expected scan shows 23, 44, 5c and 62.

Check with: sudo i2cdetect -y 1 (apt-get install i2c-tools)

The serial device can be overridden with the PMS7003_DEVICE environment variable.

# Maintenance timers at midnight
To keep the daily jobs inside the quiet window, on each node a drop-in moves
the timers to midnight (apt-daily-upgrade to 00:30), without random delay:

 for t in apt-daily man-db fstrim logrotate e2scrub_all rpi-zram-writeback \
          systemd-tmpfiles-clean dpkg-db-backup; do
   sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/$t.timer.d
   printf '[Timer]\nOnCalendar=\nOnBootSec=\nOnUnitActiveSec=\nOnCalendar=*-*-* 00:00:00\nRandomizedDelaySec=0\nAccuracySec=1min\nPersistent=false\n' \
     | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/$t.timer.d/midnight.conf
 done
 (same for apt-daily-upgrade.timer with 00:30:00)
 sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Dependencies
Installed netconfd and yangcli
 apt-get install netconfd yangcli libyuma-dev

# Installation
 autoreconf -i -f
 ./configure CFLAGS="-g -O0"  CXXFLAGS="-g -O0" --prefix=/usr
 make
 make install

# Testing installation

 netconfd --module=hs-sensor-shield --no-startup --superuser=$USER

Other terminal:
 yangcli --server=localhost --user=$USER

yangcli pi@localhost> xget /hardware
...
  data {
    hardware {
      last-change 2026-08-28T15:00:00Z
      component raspberry-pi {
        name raspberry-pi
        class ianahw:container
        serial-num 1000000020eec729
        mfg-name 'Raspberry Pi'
        model-name '4 Model B Rev 1.5'
      }
      component temperature {
        name temperature
        class ianahw:sensor
        parent raspberry-pi
        mfg-name Sensirion
        model-name SHT41
        sensor-data {
          value 23450
          value-type celsius
          value-scale milli
          value-precision 2
          oper-status ok
          value-timestamp 2026-08-28T15:00:01Z
          value-update-rate 1000
          units-display 'milli degrees'
        }
      }
      component humidity { ... }
      component pm1 { ... }
      component pm25 { ... }
      component pm10 { ... }
      component co2 { ... }
      component voc-index { ... }
      component nox-index { ... }
      component pressure { ... }
      component illuminance { ... }
    }
  }
